## What this session did

**GPT's stop-ship item 6A is fixed. Build 0.11.402, srcversion `1112F9EDFD340DB0379A7F7`, compiles clean. Still must NOT be boarded** — seven of GPT's eight items remain open.

**Corrected sess57's read of 6A.** GPT called it "mount never returns"; the code says otherwise. `mxfs_dlm_caw_lock`'s wait loop (`dlm_caw.c:2519`) breaks at `MXFS_CAW_WAIT_TIMEOUT_MS`=120 s unless the liveness oracle says every blocker is heartbeating — and a residue slot is frozen by definition — then returns `-ETIMEDOUT` (`dlm_caw.c:3147`). So the real shape is *each* blocked acquire inside `xfs_log_mount_finish` stalls 120 s and then fails the mount. Still a hard RULE-0 failure, but the fix only has to refuse a known-doomed path, not break an unbreakable one.

**Found a separate, code-proven defect on the way.** `v5_pr_fence_dead_node` returned `false` only on `-ESTALE`. Every other error from `mxfs_scsipr_fence_node` — including the READ KEYS failure that function itself logs as *"cannot classify; skipping preempt"* — was reported to callers as a **successful fence**, and they then replayed that node's journal slice. That is exactly what every comment on the path says must never happen to a node that may still be writing. Split into `v5_pr_fence_dead_node_rc()` returning the errno; the two deliberate zero-returns for topologies that can't do per-node PR still count as fenced, since D1+lease carry fencing there. This defect is what makes 6A's residue path reachable in practice, so the two fixes are load-bearing together.

**The 6A fix itself**, five edits: a read-only batched CAW census (`mxfs_dlm_caw_footprint_scan`, `-EIO` on any unreadable slot — an incomplete census is never a zero); a bounded 5×1 s fence retry breaking early on the terminal self-fence verdict; a `mount_residue_blocking` predicate; a new barrier step (b2) that returns `-EIO` when the unfenceable residue still owns resources; and a call site that fails the mount via `out_free_metadir` (verified correct unwind — `m_metadirip` NULL, `rip` not yet held).

**Item 1 analysed, fix designed, not yet applied.** `mxfs_blkdev_flush_epoch`'s skip-under-`fua_disable` rests on a coherency argument plus a per-metadata-op cost argument. Neither covers the barrier, which needs durability: publishing "slice recovered" and purging the manifest while the recovered buffers sit in the target's write cache loses both on a target power loss. Next session adds `mxfs_blkdev_flush_durable(mp)` for the barrier and publication paths, leaving the per-modify callers alone.

**Criteria: NO.** Not production ready — 11 open defects, 4 critical; defect #1's step-4a work has 7 of GPT's 8 stop-ship items outstanding and no rig cycle has been spent yet.

